1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,195
Welcome to part two: production patterns.

2
00:00:03,476 --> 00:00:05,012
We start with unit tests.

3
00:00:05,292 --> 00:00:11,798
Our tests so far check the data, but our business logic could have a bug that today's data simply does not show.

4
00:00:12,078 --> 00:00:13,486
Unit tests catch that.

5
00:00:14,586 --> 00:00:15,866
Two different questions.

6
00:00:16,146 --> 00:00:20,477
A data test runs on the real tables and asks: is this data right?

7
00:00:20,757 --> 00:00:26,474
A unit test feeds a model a few rows that we invent, and asks: does my SQL do what I meant?

8
00:00:27,574 --> 00:00:32,374
The unit tests page, version one, available since dbt one point eight.

9
00:00:32,654 --> 00:00:34,404
A unit test lives in YAML.

10
00:00:34,684 --> 00:00:38,737
Given: fake input rows for every model the tested model reads.

11
00:00:39,017 --> 00:00:41,001
Expect: the rows that should come out.

12
00:00:41,281 --> 00:00:43,180
You only list the columns that matter.

13
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:45,432
One unit test.

14
00:00:45,712 --> 00:00:48,720
The model under test is the intermediate trips model.

15
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,592
Given one fake trip with zero distance, and empty inputs for the two seeds, because every input must be mocked.

16
00:00:55,872 --> 00:01:01,056
We expect fare per mile to be empty, the trip invalid, and the reason zero distance.

17
00:01:02,156 --> 00:01:04,673
Deciding what to test is the human part.

18
00:01:04,953 --> 00:01:07,214
Four cases from the problems we found.

19
00:01:07,494 --> 00:01:08,604
Zero distance.

20
00:01:08,884 --> 00:01:10,825
A trip that ends before it starts.

21
00:01:11,105 --> 00:01:16,758
A normal trip, two and a half miles for fifteen dollars, which must be valid at six dollars a mile.

22
00:01:17,038 --> 00:01:18,382
And a negative total.

23
00:01:19,482 --> 00:01:28,229
In the demo, we describe the four cases in plain English, and the agent writes the YAML, mocks every input, and runs only the unit tests.

24
00:01:28,509 --> 00:01:30,984
Verbose YAML is perfect agent work.

25
00:01:31,264 --> 00:01:32,586
The cases are ours.

26
00:01:33,686 --> 00:01:36,225
We run only the unit tests, and four pass.

27
00:01:36,505 --> 00:01:40,665
If one fails, ask the honest question: is the test wrong, or the model?

28
00:01:41,765 --> 00:01:45,135
A test you have never seen fail is a test you cannot trust.

29
00:01:45,415 --> 00:01:47,207
So we break the model on purpose.

30
00:01:48,307 --> 00:01:53,043
We change one character: less than or equal to zero becomes less than zero.

31
00:01:53,323 --> 00:01:55,329
Now zero-distance trips are valid.

32
00:01:55,609 --> 00:02:00,793
The first unit test turns red, and dbt shows what we expected and what we got.

33
00:02:01,073 --> 00:02:03,547
We undo the change, and it is green again.
